  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68117,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 229,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "<p>Lipofibroadenoma of the thymus is localized in the anterior (prevascular) mediastinum.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-01-04T11:36:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68118,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 229,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "<p>Of the 6&nbsp;patients reported so far, 3&nbsp;were asymptomatic and 2&nbsp;presented with cough (one with fever and the other with expectoration). The remaining patient, in whom lipofibroadenoma was associated with type&nbsp;B1 thymoma, had dyspnoea and pure red cell aplasia {11469342}. All cases reported were&nbsp;TNM stage&nbsp;I tumours.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T15:48:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68119,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 229,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "<p>Only 6&nbsp;tumours&nbsp;have ever been reported, all in men aged 20&ndash;62&nbsp;years {11469342; 19425382; 23856156; 20021971; 26234840; 30303172}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T15:49:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68120,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 229,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-10-27T09:59:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68121,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 229,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "<p>It is uncertain whether the epithelial or the lipofibromatous component (or both) is neoplastic, or whether the lesion is a hamartoma. Because the tumours in 3&nbsp;reported cases were associated with an adjacent type&nbsp;B1 thymoma {11469342; 20021971; 30303172}, a putative thymic epithelial cell precursor is hypothesized to contribute to tumour development.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T15:49:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68122,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 229,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumours are oval and well circumscribed, with maximal diameters ranging from 30 to 230 mm. The cut surface is solid, grey, and firm.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-03T11:07:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68123,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 229,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumour resembles fibroadenoma of the breast. Fibrotic and hyaline stroma predominates over narrow strands of bland-looking epithelial cells, single or multiple fat cells, and few lymphocytes. Hassall corpuscles (reminiscent of thymic parenchyma) and calcifications can occur. Three cases arose adjacent to a type&nbsp;B1 thymoma {11469342; 20021971; 30303172}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>The epithelial cells are positive for AE1/AE3 and CK19. Lymphocytes express either CD3 or CD20, but not TdT.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Thymolipoma is characterized by a predominance of fatty tissue over epithelial cell strands with or without (usually minimal) thymic parenchyma and absence of a fibrous tissue component {20107952}. Thymofibrolipoma has been described as a subtype of thymolipoma with a substantial fibrous component {8135632}. It is currently unclear whether thymolipoma, thymofibrolipoma, and lipofibroadenoma constitute a spectrum of neoplastic or hamartomatous entities.</p>\r\n<p>Sclerosing thymomas are distinguished by the presence of large islands of polygonal cells in a dense collagenous stroma without intermixed adipose tissue. Tumour cell palisades around perivascular spaces and lymphocytes may occur.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-01-19T17:52:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
